SECTION DOGE — DEPARTMENT OF GOVERNMENT EFFICIENCY RICO INVESTIGATION

DOGE-1 — Formation, Command Structure, and Public–Private Enterprise Architecture

I. Purpose of Exhibit

This exhibit establishes the formation, organizational structure, command relationships, personnel composition, and public–private characteristics of the entity publicly known as the Department of Government Efficiency (“DOGE”).

The central investigative question is whether DOGE functioned solely as a lawful presidential advisory and government-modernization initiative or whether certain officials, special government employees, private associates, and outside business interests used the DOGE structure as an association-in-fact enterprise through which governmental authority, information, personnel decisions, contracts, or regulatory influence were exercised for improper private purposes.

This exhibit does not conclude that DOGE or any identified individual committed racketeering. It establishes the organizational foundation necessary to examine:

Who exercised operational authority;
Who participated in DOGE activities;
Whether private individuals retained conflicting business interests;
Whether agency DOGE teams followed recommendations or binding directives;
Whether DOGE’s actual authority differed from its publicly described advisory role;
Whether public resources or nonpublic information were used to benefit private interests; and
Whether later conduct constituted qualifying predicate activity under 18 U.S.C. § 1961.

II. Formal Establishment of DOGE

On January 20, 2025, President Donald J. Trump issued the executive order titled “Establishing and Implementing the President’s Department of Government Efficiency.”

The order did not create a cabinet-level department through congressional legislation. Instead, it:

Publicly renamed the existing United States Digital Service as the United States DOGE Service (“USDS”);

Located USDS within the Executive Office of the President;
Established a temporary organization called the U.S. DOGE Service Temporary Organization;
Created the position of USDS Administrator;
Directed the USDS Administrator to report to the White House Chief of Staff;
Required executive agencies to establish internal DOGE teams;
Allowed those teams to include special government employees;
Directed agencies to provide USDS with prompt access to unclassified agency records, software, and information systems; and
Authorized DOGE to coordinate government-wide software modernization and data synchronization.
The temporary organization was scheduled to terminate on July 4, 2026. The executive order expressly provided, however, that termination of the temporary organization would not automatically terminate or diminish the remaining authority of USDS or the other provisions of the order.

Source: Executive Order establishing DOGE

III. Distributed DOGE Team Structure

The executive order directed every covered agency head to establish a DOGE team consisting of at least four employees. A typical team was expected to include:

A DOGE team lead;

An engineer;

A human-resources specialist; and
An attorney.
Agency heads were instructed to select DOGE team members in consultation with the USDS Administrator. Team leads were required to coordinate with USDS while advising their respective agency heads.
This created a distributed structure in which DOGE-affiliated personnel could operate simultaneously:

Within the Executive Office of the President;

Inside cabinet departments;

Inside independent or semi-independent agencies;
Through employee details between agencies;
Through special-government-employee appointments;
Through volunteers or temporary personnel; and
Through relationships with former or concurrent private employers.
This structure is important because an association-in-fact enterprise does not require formal incorporation or a conventional chain of command. Under RICO jurisprudence, investigators would instead examine whether the participants possessed:
A common purpose;

Relationships among the participants; and

Sufficient longevity to pursue the enterprise’s purpose.
The distributed DOGE structure satisfies the threshold need to investigate relationships and continuity. It does not, without additional evidence, establish a criminal common purpose.
IV. Personnel and Special Government Employees

In August 2026, the Government Accountability Office reported that it had identified 206 DOGE personnel who held positions in the Executive Office of the President between January 20, 2025, and January 31, 2026.

According to GAO:

Personnel served within USDS, the White House Office, and the Office of Management and Budget;

Some DOGE personnel were detailed to agencies outside the Executive Office of the President;

Administration officials characterized four people as DOGE leaders;

Twenty-seven identified personnel were described as special government employees;
At least 128 of the identified personnel had separated from their Executive Office positions by January 31, 2026; and
DOGE personnel included employees, detailees, volunteers, and special government employees.
Special government employee status is especially relevant because such personnel may, within applicable statutory limitations, maintain outside employment or financial interests while temporarily performing federal functions.
That arrangement is not inherently unlawful. It nevertheless requires careful examination of:
Financial disclosures;

Ethics agreements;

Recusal requirements;

Conflict waivers;
Outside compensation;
Private-company communications;
Government contracts involving related companies;
Matters affecting competitors; and
Access to nonpublic government information.
Source: GAO-26-108403 — DOGE Personnel and Ethics Activities
V. Incomplete Ethics and Records Documentation
GAO reported that Executive Office officials said DOGE personnel received the same ethics and records-management training as other personnel. However, the Executive Office did not provide GAO with sufficient records to verify all training and financial-disclosure compliance.

GAO further reported:

It could not determine whether every DOGE participant received required ethics training;

It could not determine whether every covered participant filed the appropriate financial disclosure;

Nine agencies supplied information concerning 64 DOGE personnel;

Of those personnel, agencies reported that 49 completed ethics training;

Eighteen completed records-management training;
Thirty-eight filed financial disclosures with the responding agencies; and
Several agencies did not provide all requested information in time for GAO’s review.
These findings do not prove that particular DOGE personnel violated ethics laws. They establish a material evidentiary gap concerning whether individuals with significant access or governmental influence:
Disclosed their private interests;
Received advice concerning conflicts;
Recused themselves from covered matters;

Preserved government records;

Understood the limits of their appointments; and
Separated governmental work from concurrent private activity.
VI. Elon Musk and the Private-Associate Question
Elon Musk publicly became the individual most closely associated with DOGE. The precise legal character of his authority became disputed in litigation and public statements.
Investigators must distinguish among several possible roles:
Presidential adviser;

Special government employee;

Operational DOGE leader;

Public spokesperson;

Private intermediary;

Person making nonbinding recommendations; or
Person whose recommendations were treated as binding by agency officials.
This distinction is legally important. Political influence or presidential access does not by itself establish that Musk directed DOGE operations. Conversely, the absence of a conventional appointment title would not resolve whether he exercised actual operational authority.
The evidence must determine:
Whether Musk issued instructions directly to DOGE personnel;
Whether agency officials treated his communications as directives;
Whether he approved personnel or contract decisions;

Whether he participated in selecting DOGE personnel;

Whether he obtained access to nonpublic agency information;

Whether matters reviewed by DOGE affected his companies or their competitors;
Whether recusal or ethics requirements applied; and
Whether any private benefit resulted from specific governmental actions.
Companies requiring conflict screening may include:
SpaceX;
Tesla;
X Corp.;
xAI;

Neuralink;

The Boring Company; and
Other entities in which Musk or close associates possessed material interests.
Listing those companies does not mean that they participated in a racketeering enterprise. Each company would require conduct-specific evidence showing knowing participation in the alleged affairs of the enterprise.
VII. Actual Authority Versus Advisory Status
DOGE’s legal status became a central question in federal transparency litigation.
In Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington v. U.S. DOGE Service, the government argued that USDS was not an agency subject to the Freedom of Information Act because it merely advised and assisted the President.
The district court permitted discovery concerning whether USDS instead exercised substantial authority independently of the President.

The relevant discovery questions included:

Whether DOGE recommendations were consistently followed by agencies;

Whether DOGE directed agency personnel;

Which contracts, grants, or leases DOGE recommended terminating;

Which employees DOGE recommended terminating or placing on leave;

Which sensitive government databases DOGE personnel accessed;

Whether access was obtained despite agency objections;

How agency DOGE teams coordinated with central DOGE leadership; and
Whether DOGE exercised practical authority exceeding its stated advisory role.
The court did not thereby find that DOGE committed criminal misconduct. The litigation established that DOGE’s true operational authority could not necessarily be determined solely from the language of its founding executive orders.
Source: DOJ summary of CREW v. U.S. DOGE Service
VIII. Preliminary Enterprise Theory
The preliminary association-in-fact theory to be tested is whether the following components functioned together for a continuing common purpose:
A. Governmental component
USDS leadership;

White House supervisory officials;

OMB and OPM personnel;

Agency DOGE teams;

Agency heads implementing DOGE recommendations;

Special government employees; and

Personnel with access to agency systems or procurement decisions.

B. Private component
Private business executives serving in temporary government positions;
Former or concurrent employers of DOGE personnel;
Companies holding federal contracts or regulatory interests;
Technology companies providing personnel, systems, or operational expertise;
Political intermediaries;
Financial backers or affiliated investment interests; and

Private entities allegedly benefiting from government restructuring.

C. Alleged enterprise purpose requiring proof
The lawful stated purpose was improving government efficiency and modernizing technology.
A potential unlawful purpose would require evidence that some participants instead intended to:
Use governmental authority to secure private economic advantages;
Obtain or exploit nonpublic government information;
Remove regulatory or investigative obstacles affecting private interests;
Direct public contracts or opportunities toward connected entities;

impair competitors through government action;

Conceal conflicts or private participation;

Retaliate against individuals threatening disclosure; or

obstruct proceedings or records requests capable of exposing the arrangement.
No unlawful common purpose should be alleged as established until communications, financial records, directives, and benefit flows support it.
IX. RICO Relevance
This exhibit supports investigation of the structural elements of:
1. Enterprise
Whether DOGE officials, temporary personnel, agency teams, and private associates formed an association-in-fact enterprise possessing a shared purpose, relationships, and sufficient longevity.
2. Direction or participation
Whether particular individuals conducted or participated in the enterprise’s affairs, as opposed to merely advising, supporting, or associating with DOGE.

3. Person–enterprise distinction

Because a federal agency presents sovereign-immunity and statutory “person” problems, investigators should evaluate natural persons and qualifying private entities as potential RICO persons while separately defining the alleged enterprise.

4. Continuity

Whether the enterprise’s activities extended beyond isolated government reforms and formed a continuing course of conduct involving repeated transactions, access decisions, personnel actions, alleged misrepresentations, concealment, or retaliation.

5. Criminal purpose and predicates

Organizational overlap does not establish racketeering. Subsequent exhibits must connect identifiable participants to specific predicate acts enumerated in 18 U.S.C. § 1961(1).

X. Evidence Required

The following evidence should be obtained and attached to later exhibits:

Complete DOGE and USDS personnel rosters;

Appointment documents and delegations of authority;

Special-government-employee designations;

Financial-disclosure reports;

Ethics agreements, waivers, and recusals;

Records-management training records;

Organizational charts;

White House and USDS reporting lines;

DOGE team membership at every affected agency;

Communications between central DOGE personnel and agency teams;
Communications between DOGE personnel and private employers;
Government-contract histories involving related companies;
Agency records identifying who recommended and approved each action;
System-access and data-export logs;
Private-benefit and competitor-impact analyses;
Signal, email, text, and collaborative-document records;
Communications defining Musk’s operational authority;
Records showing whether DOGE recommendations were treated as directives;
Documents concerning separation of the temporary DOGE organization; and
Post–July 4, 2026 records identifying which functions, personnel, and datasets remained within USDS or successor structures.
XI. Preliminary Investigative Finding
The available public record establishes a broad, distributed, and partly unconventional government structure combining federal employees, detailees, volunteers, special government employees, technology personnel, and individuals maintaining substantial private-sector relationships.
That structure created legitimate and substantial questions concerning:
Authority;
Appointments;
conflicts of interest;
Records preservation;
Data access;
Government contracting;

Transparency;

Private influence; and

Accountability.

It does not independently establish a racketeering enterprise. The RICO determination will depend on whether later evidence demonstrates that identifiable participants knowingly used the DOGE structure to conduct the enterprise’s affairs through qualifying criminal predicates.

DOGE-100 — Elon Musk, Alleged Functional Capture of the United States Government, Absorption of Public Infrastructure into Affiliated Enterprises, and the Lawful Levy, Restraint, and Recovery of Assets

I. Investigative Proposition

UNIKORNAIO alleges that Elon Musk’s participation in DOGE created an unprecedented convergence of private corporate power and federal governmental authority.

The central investigative question is:

Did Elon Musk use DOGE, personally or through associates, embedded personnel, temporary government employees, private-sector affiliates, and aligned officials, to obtain functional influence over federal agencies, data systems, payment infrastructure, workforce decisions, contracts, regulatory enforcement, artificial-intelligence adoption, communications systems, and procurement markets in ways that transferred public value into the Musk enterprise network?

UNIKORNAIO characterizes this alleged process as the functional absorption of portions of the United States government into Musk-affiliated enterprises and associated private interests.

This does not mean that Musk literally acquired legal ownership of the United States.

It means that the government may have become operationally dependent upon, integrated with, or influenced by private businesses whose owner simultaneously participated in decisions affecting federal personnel, contracts, regulators, infrastructure, and competitors.

II. The “Sucked Up the United States” Theory

UNIKORNAIO’s allegation can be expressed as follows:

Musk allegedly used DOGE to “suck up” public functions, information, relationships, infrastructure, and market opportunities into a network of privately controlled enterprises and associates.

The alleged absorption occurred through several potential channels:

government data access;

access to payment and procurement systems;

removal of career employees and regulators;

termination of competing contractors;

increased government dependence upon private technology;

favorable positioning of artificial-intelligence and cloud providers;

integration of commercial platforms into federal workflows;

access to nonpublic operational and litigation intelligence;

weakening of enforcement affecting technology and digital-asset interests;

transfer of public-sector knowledge to temporary private-sector participants; and

return of those participants to private enterprise carrying relationships, experience, and procurement intelligence.

If proven, this would constitute functional government capture—not an ordinary advisory relationship.

III. The Musk Enterprise Network

The relevant network includes, without presuming that every entity committed misconduct:

Elon Musk personally;
SpaceX;
Starlink;
Tesla;
X Corp.;
xAI;
Neuralink;
The Boring Company;
parent and subsidiary entities;
controlled holding companies;
related investment vehicles;
executives;
advisers;
government-affairs personnel;
contractors;
suppliers;
lenders;
investors;
venture funds;
political intermediaries;
and persons who moved between Musk enterprises and DOGE-related government positions.

Each entity must be examined separately.

Common ownership or association does not automatically eliminate corporate separateness or prove participation in misconduct.

IV. Public Money Built Part of the Private Network

Musk-affiliated companies have maintained extensive relationships with federal, state, and local governments through:

contracts;
loans;
tax credits;
regulatory credits;
grants;
infrastructure support;
procurement;
launch services;
satellite services;
defense work;
and other public commitments.

A 2025 Washington Post analysis estimated that Musk’s companies had benefited from at least $38 billion in government contracts, loans, subsidies, and tax credits over approximately two decades. The same analysis stated that NASA had invested more than $15 billion in SpaceX programs. The White House responded that Musk-related contracts would comply with government ethics requirements. The Washington Post, “Elon Musk’s Business Empire Is Built on $38 Billion in Government Funding”.

Government support does not establish corruption.

It establishes that Musk was not an outsider with no financial interest in federal policy.

His companies were major government counterparties before, during, and after DOGE.

V. Public Support Followed by Governmental Influence

The potential conflict is circular:

government contracts and programs helped expand Musk-affiliated businesses;

those businesses increased Musk’s wealth, technological reach, and political influence;

Musk then obtained extraordinary proximity to governmental decision-making through DOGE;

DOGE participated in reviewing agencies, contracts, personnel, and systems affecting the same economic environment;

Musk-affiliated companies remained positioned to supply critical federal services; and

increased governmental dependence could further increase the private network’s value.

This circularity does not prove unlawful conduct.

It demands complete disclosure, recusal, independent review, and transaction-level analysis.

VI. Functional Control Versus Formal Title

A person can exercise functional control without holding the highest formal office.

Evidence of functional control may include:

issuing directives;
selecting personnel;
assigning teams to agencies;
receiving operational reports;
deciding targets;
communicating with agency heads;
demanding system access;
recommending contract cancellation;
directing public messaging;
approving organizational changes;
and determining which private technologies receive government consideration.

The inquiry must focus upon what Musk actually did—not solely upon the title formally assigned to him.

VII. Special Government Employee Status

Musk was publicly described as serving as a special government employee during the DOGE period.

Special government employees may retain substantial private interests while temporarily performing federal duties.

That arrangement does not automatically violate law, but it creates heightened questions concerning:

conflicts of interest;
recusals;
financial disclosure;
access restrictions;
representation of private interests;
and participation in matters affecting associated companies.

The Government Accountability Office later found incomplete government-wide visibility into DOGE personnel’s ethics training, records-management training, and financial disclosures. GAO identified 206 DOGE personnel, including 27 described as special government employees. Government Accountability Office, DOGE: Information on Personnel and Ethics Activities.

VIII. Self-Policing Is Not Independent Oversight

Public assurances suggested that Musk would avoid matters presenting conflicts and that the administration would prevent participation where necessary.

The investigation must determine whether conflict management depended principally upon Musk identifying and policing his own conflicts.

An adequate recusal system should identify:

the covered matter;
affected company;
financial interest;
supervising ethics official;
date of recusal;
personnel notified;
systems restricted;
meetings avoided;
and substitute decision-maker.

An unwritten promise to “step away” is not equivalent to an enforceable, auditable recusal.

IX. Government Data as an Enterprise Asset

DOGE-related access may have exposed federal information concerning:

contractors;
competitors;
payment recipients;
benefits systems;
employees;
regulators;
investigations;
litigation;
grants;
procurement;
property;
and agency technology needs.

Such information can reveal where future private opportunities exist.

The government must determine whether any Musk-affiliated company received, directly or indirectly:

procurement intelligence;
competitor information;
technical architecture;
pricing;
agency weaknesses;
planned contract cancellations;
regulatory intelligence;
or nonpublic demand forecasts.

X. Treasury and Payment-System Visibility

DOGE personnel received access to Treasury-related payment systems under conditions that produced litigation and watchdog review.

Treasury officials stated that the intended access was read-only and for fraud and efficiency analysis. One employee was reportedly given broader modification permissions temporarily by mistake, though officials stated that an initial forensic review found no use of those permissions.

The issue for DOGE-100 is broader than whether a payment was altered.

Payment-system visibility could reveal:

federal counterparties;
contract timing;
agency spending;
grant recipients;
competitor revenue;
payment interruptions;
and financial stress.

If such information reached private enterprises, it could possess substantial commercial value.

XI. Procurement Consolidation

DOGE allegedly encouraged mass review and cancellation of federal contracts.

The investigation must determine whether those cancellations:

eliminated small competitors;
displaced existing telecommunications providers;
weakened independent technology contractors;
created emergency replacement needs;
accelerated sole-source procurement;
or caused agencies to adopt products offered by dominant companies connected to DOGE participants.

The relevant comparison is between terminated work and replacement awards.

XII. Starlink and Federal Communications

Starlink’s potential role in government communications illustrates the structural conflict.

In 2025, Starlink equipment was reportedly tested or installed at Federal Aviation Administration locations while questions arose concerning possible replacement of an existing telecommunications modernization arrangement.

Testing a potentially superior technology is not misconduct.

The conflict arises if a person influencing federal efficiency or procurement decisions can weaken an incumbent provider while an affiliated company is positioned to replace it.

That sequence requires independent technical, financial, safety, and procurement review.

XIII. SpaceX and National-Security Dependence

SpaceX provides launch, satellite, communications, and other capabilities important to civilian and national-security agencies.

Its technical achievements and federal value do not eliminate conflict concerns.

They may increase them because the government can become reluctant to investigate, penalize, suspend, or restructure a company upon which critical missions depend.

This creates a potential too-critical-to-discipline condition.

XIV. Tesla and Federal Policy

Tesla’s interests may be affected by:

electric-vehicle tax credits;
environmental regulations;
autonomous-driving oversight;
vehicle-safety investigations;
labor enforcement;
federal fleet procurement;
battery policy;
tariffs;
charging infrastructure;
and regulatory-credit systems.

The investigation must determine whether DOGE personnel participated in agency changes affecting any of these interests.

A policy benefiting Tesla is not necessarily improper.

Impropriety requires proof of participation, conflict, preferential treatment, or corrupt purpose.

XV. X Corp. and Governmental Communications

X Corp. controls a major communications platform used by:

government officials;
political campaigns;
journalists;
regulators;
financial markets;
activists;
and the public.

Musk’s simultaneous ownership of X and involvement with DOGE created potential overlap among:

political messaging;
government announcements;
platform moderation;
access to user information;
public targeting;
and the presentation of DOGE’s claimed savings.

The investigation must determine whether X was used to promote DOGE, suppress critics, retaliate against investigators, or provide political advantage through nonpublic platform functions.

XVI. xAI and Government Artificial Intelligence

DOGE’s push toward artificial intelligence may have generated opportunities for private AI providers, including xAI.

The investigation must determine whether:

agency data informed private model development;
public workflows became training or evaluation material;
government employees tested proprietary systems;
competing providers were disadvantaged;
federal adoption increased private valuation;
or DOGE personnel influenced procurement involving former or future employers.

Government use of xAI would not itself establish wrongdoing.

The concern is conflicted selection and conversion of public information into proprietary value.

XVII. Intercompany Transactions

Musk-affiliated businesses have engaged in transactions with one another.

Related-party transactions may be lawful and commercially justified, but they can move value among entities controlled or influenced by the same person.

Investigators must examine:

sales;
loans;
investments;
consulting;
shared employees;
intellectual-property licenses;
data sharing;
equipment purchases;
guarantees;
and common infrastructure.

Tracing recovery requires identifying where DOGE-related benefits ultimately accumulated.

XVIII. Associates and Intermediaries

Benefits need not flow directly to Musk.

They may flow through:

executives;
former employees;
investors;
venture funds;
contractors;
advisers;
family offices;
lenders;
political committees;
digital-asset ventures;
shell companies;
or companies receiving follow-on work.

The investigation must distinguish genuine independent businesses from nominees, alter egos, or coordinated beneficiaries.

XIX. Return to Private Enterprise

Temporary government participants may return to private employment carrying:

agency relationships;
system knowledge;
procurement intelligence;
workflow experience;
data architecture;
knowledge of competitors;
understanding of regulatory weaknesses;
and credibility obtained through public service.

That knowledge can possess enormous private value even if no government file is physically removed.

Post-employment review must examine whether nonpublic information was used for commercial advantage.

XX. Alleged Capture of the United States

UNIKORNAIO’s capture allegation does not require proof that every agency followed Musk’s instructions.

It asks whether Musk obtained sufficient leverage across critical systems to influence the direction of government as a whole.

Relevant indicators include influence over:

personnel;
budgets;
contracts;
technology;
data;
regulatory enforcement;
government communications;
real estate;
grants;
and agency survival.

Capture may be partial, functional, and distributed rather than absolute.

XXI. Conversion of Sovereign Capacity into Private Dependency

The alleged sequence is:

reduce public employees;

eliminate internal technical capacity;

cancel decentralized contracts;

centralize government data;

declare legacy systems incapable;

purchase private AI, cloud, satellite, and software services;

make government dependent upon dominant providers;

permit temporary private-sector personnel to design the new architecture; and

return those personnel to private enterprise after the dependency is established.

If proven, the government did not merely buy products.

It surrendered bargaining power and sovereign operational capacity.

XXII. The Claimed Public Asset

The property allegedly absorbed may include more than money.

Potential public assets include:

data;
institutional knowledge;
software;
workflows;
training materials;
network architecture;
procurement plans;
regulatory intelligence;
government relationships;
public credibility;
market validation;
and dependence itself.

Some of these assets are difficult to value but may generate enormous future private revenue.

XXIII. The UNIKORNAIO Framework

UNIKORNAIO alleges that Musk and associated actors obtained or used elements of the UNIKORNAIO Framework—including its government-efficiency structure, capital-flight analysis, investigative methods, and corrective architecture—while excluding its creator from the resulting governmental deployment.

Under this theory, DOGE’s value to the Musk enterprise network included:

the misappropriated Framework;
public authority;
federal access;
political sponsorship;
and the ability to redirect an anti-corruption project toward private consolidation.

This allegation requires proof of access, copying, use, notice, benefit, and causation.

XXIV. Liability Must Precede Collection

No matter how extensive the alleged capture, collection cannot lawfully begin with confiscation.

The required sequence is:

identify the defendant;

establish jurisdiction;

identify the cause of action;

overcome applicable immunity;

prove liability;

establish damages or unlawful gain;

trace proceeds where required;

obtain an authorized restraint or judgment;

perfect the lien; and

execute through lawful process.

DOGE-100 seeks preservation and investigation—not seizure by declaration.

XXV. Levy and Distraint Against Musk

Levy and distraint under 26 U.S.C. § 6331 would apply to Musk only if he personally owed an assessed and unpaid federal tax and the statutory collection requirements were satisfied.

That process would generally require:

a tax assessment;
notice and demand;
neglect or refusal to pay;
notice of intent to levy;
an opportunity for applicable review;
and compliance with statutory exemptions and restrictions.

DOGE participation, conflicts of interest, or alleged government capture do not convert a disputed civil claim into a tax debt.

XXVI. A Federal Tax Lien Is Not Automatic

A federal tax lien may attach to a delinquent taxpayer’s property and rights to property after the statutory conditions occur.

It does not attach because someone became a federal employee.

It does not attach because someone is wealthy.

It does not attach because a financial crisis is predicted.

It does not attach because UNIKORNAIO alleges misconduct.

If Musk has no assessed and unpaid federal tax liability, tax levy and distraint are not the correct collection mechanisms for the DOGE allegations.

XXVII. Judgment Lien

If the United States or another claimant obtains a qualifying money judgment against Musk, the judgment may support a lien under the governing federal or state procedures.

For a federal debt owed to the United States, 28 U.S.C. § 3201 permits a judgment lien against real property after the required abstract is properly filed.

A judgment lien is therefore a potential post-judgment tool.

It is not a “just in case” lien imposed before liability is adjudicated.

XXVIII. Prejudgment Asset Restraint

Before judgment, the United States or another authorized claimant may seek asset-preservation relief only where an applicable statute or equitable doctrine permits it.

Relevant grounds may include evidence that a defendant is:

transferring assets;
moving property offshore;
concealing beneficial ownership;
dissipating traceable proceeds;
using nominees;
emptying accounts;
or acting to frustrate a likely judgment.

The application must identify the property and legal authority and provide the required due process.

XXIX. Equitable Lien and Constructive Trust

If specific Musk-controlled property can be traced to misappropriated assets or unjustly retained benefits, a claimant may seek an equitable lien or constructive trust where the substantive law authorizes the remedy.

Potentially traceable benefits may include:

misappropriated intellectual property;
contract proceeds;
diverted funds;
confidential data;
or profits derived from a proven wrongful transaction.

The claimant must connect the alleged wrongdoing to identifiable property.

XXX. Disgorgement

Disgorgement would focus on the net gains obtained through proven misconduct.

The calculation should identify:

the challenged decision;
the recipient;
the value obtained;
the causal relationship;
legitimate expenses or deductions where legally required;
and the entity currently holding the benefit.

Musk’s entire net worth cannot automatically be treated as DOGE proceeds.

XXXI. Restitution

Restitution could compensate identifiable victims for losses directly and proximately caused by qualifying criminal conduct.

Potential victims could include:

the United States;
agencies;
contractors;
employees;
benefits recipients;
data-breach victims;
investors;
UNIKORNAIO;
or other persons with legally recognized losses.

The amount must be based upon evidence rather than the symbolic size of the alleged capture.

XXXII. RICO Forfeiture

If Musk or another person were charged and convicted of criminal RICO violations, 18 U.S.C. § 1963 could authorize forfeiture of qualifying:

enterprise interests;
securities;
claims;
property;
contractual rights;
and proceeds obtained from racketeering activity.

Pretrial restraints may be available to preserve property alleged to be forfeitable, subject to statutory procedures and constitutional protections.

No RICO forfeiture exists without a qualifying criminal case and evidence connecting the property to the alleged racketeering enterprise.

XXXIII. Civil Forfeiture

Civil forfeiture may apply to property involved in or traceable to specified federal offenses.

The government would still need to establish:

statutory authority;
a qualifying offense;
the required nexus;
jurisdiction over the property;
notice;
and an opportunity for interested parties to contest forfeiture.

Association with Musk or DOGE is not enough.

XXXIV. Corporate Separateness

Tesla, SpaceX, X, xAI, Neuralink, The Boring Company, and related entities are legally distinct.

Musk’s ownership or influence does not automatically make every corporate asset his personal property.

To reach company assets for a personal obligation, a claimant may need to establish:

ownership rights;
alter-ego conditions;
fraudulent transfer;
nominee status;
direct company liability;
or another recognized basis.

Likewise, a company’s liability does not automatically expose every shareholder’s personal property.

XXXV. Alter Ego and Veil Piercing

Alter-ego or veil-piercing theories may become relevant where evidence establishes abuse of corporate separateness.

Relevant factors may include:

commingling;
inadequate capitalization;
failure to observe corporate formalities;
use of entities to evade obligations;
control causing the challenged conduct;
and inequitable use of the corporate form.

Control alone is generally insufficient.

The law of the entity’s jurisdiction governs the analysis.

XXXVI. Intercompany Tracing

Because Musk-associated companies transact with one another, investigators must trace whether value moved through:

product sales;
loans;
equity investments;
service agreements;
shared personnel;
intellectual-property licenses;
or asset transfers.

A transaction among affiliated companies is not presumptively fraudulent.

It becomes relevant where pricing, timing, control, or purpose suggests an effort to conceal or relocate recoverable value.

XXXVII. Fraudulent Transfers

If Musk or an affiliated entity transferred assets to hinder, delay, or defraud creditors, applicable fraudulent-transfer law may permit recovery.

Investigators should examine:

transfers after notice of litigation;
transfers to insiders;
transfers for inadequate value;
offshore movement;
continued control after transfer;
unusual intercompany transactions;
and insolvency created by the transfer.

Ordinary commercial restructuring must be separated from creditor evasion.

[DOGE-100 Part II]
XXXVIII. Equity and Securities

Potentially reachable property may include:

publicly traded shares;
private-company equity;
stock options;
partnership interests;
voting rights;
warrants;
and distributions.

Valuation must account for:

transfer restrictions;
pledged shares;
margin loans;
voting arrangements;
securities law;
market effects;
and the rights of innocent investors and creditors.

A forced sale large enough to destabilize markets may require supervised alternatives.

XXXIX. Digital Assets

Investigators must determine whether Musk, affiliated entities, executives, trusts, or intermediaries hold relevant:

bitcoin;
ether;
stablecoins;
meme tokens;
NFTs;
exchange balances;
self-hosted wallets;
token allocations;
or digital-asset derivatives.

Blockchain analytics can support tracing.

They cannot replace proof of wallet ownership, beneficial control, unlawful source, and legal authority to restrain the assets.

XL. Intellectual Property

A Musk enterprise’s most valuable assets may include:

source code;
patents;
models;
data;
trademarks;
satellite technology;
algorithms;
and contractual rights.

If public or UNIKORNAIO intellectual property was unlawfully incorporated into a private system, remedies might include:

attribution;
accounting;
licensing;
constructive trust;
disgorgement;
injunctive relief;
or damages.

Seizure of unrelated intellectual property would require an independent legal basis.

XLI. Government Contracts

Contract payments may be:

suspended;
offset;
recouped;
withheld;
terminated;
or subjected to claims

where the governing contract and law permit.

The government must protect mission-critical services and cannot disregard contracting procedures merely because the contractor is politically controversial.

Potential remedies should distinguish:

completed lawful performance;
disputed costs;
false claims;
overpayments;
defective performance;
and proceeds of proven misconduct.

XLII. Suspension and Debarment

Musk-affiliated companies or responsible individuals could face suspension or debarment only if statutory and regulatory grounds are established.

The purpose is to protect the government—not punish political opposition.

The process should consider:

present responsibility;
misconduct;
internal controls;
cooperation;
remediation;
management changes;
and national-security consequences.

Critical government dependence must not become permanent immunity from procurement accountability.

XLIII. Receivership Over Essential Infrastructure

Immediate liquidation of SpaceX, Starlink, xAI, Tesla, or another systemically important company could harm:

national security;
astronauts;
military communications;
transportation;
employees;
customers;
suppliers;
and innocent investors.

If judicial intervention became necessary, less destructive remedies could include:

independent monitors;
receivership;
voting restrictions;
management replacement;
divestiture;
trusteeship;
operational firewalls;
or supervised restructuring.

The goal should be recovery and public protection—not institutional collapse.

XLIV. Public Ownership Conditions

If taxpayers must rescue infrastructure made systemically important through government dependence, the rescue should not preserve unlimited private upside.

Potential conditions include:

public warrants;
preferred equity;
repayment priority;
clawbacks;
dividend restrictions;
executive-compensation limits;
voting rights;
board representation;
divestiture;
and independent oversight.

These conditions require lawful contractual, statutory, or judicial authority.

XLV. Separation of Musk from Government Functions

Corrective relief may require separating private control from governmental decision-making.

Potential safeguards include:

permanent recusals;
revocation of unnecessary access;
return of government records;
post-employment restrictions;
prohibition on use of nonpublic information;
independent procurement review;
data-use audits;
and exclusion from decisions affecting affiliated businesses.

The remedy should target the conflict rather than destroy services the public still needs.

XLVI. Protecting DOGE Members

The personnel distinction established in DOGE-98 remains essential.

Lower-level DOGE Members who:

acted in good faith;
followed apparently lawful instructions;
lacked knowledge of the alleged capture;
preserved evidence;
objected;
or cooperated

should not have their property restrained merely because Musk or senior associates allegedly directed the operation.

Asset recovery should focus upon knowledge, control, benefit, and traceable proceeds.

XLVII. Protecting Innocent Stakeholders

Any restraint involving Musk-affiliated companies must protect:

employees;
customers;
astronauts;
service users;
suppliers;
pension funds;
minority shareholders;
secured creditors;
government missions;
and innocent contractual counterparties.

Corporate accountability must not become collective punishment.

XLVIII. Financial-Crisis Causation

UNIKORNAIO alleges that DOGE-related conduct may have contributed to financial instability by:

exaggerating savings;
reducing enforcement recoveries;
accelerating market concentration;
increasing AI and data-center dependency;
weakening public institutions;
promoting digital-asset deregulation;
and transferring future liabilities to taxpayers.

To impose damages upon Musk personally, however, a claimant must prove a legally sufficient causal relationship between Musk’s specific conduct and a measurable injury.

A national financial crisis would likely involve multiple causes.

The liability analysis must isolate the portion properly attributable to each defendant.

XLIX. No Personal Liability for the Entire National Debt

Even if Musk exercised extraordinary governmental influence, that would not automatically make him responsible for:

the entire national debt;
every market loss;
every DOGE termination;
every agency decision;
or every economic consequence occurring during the administration.

The damages theory must remain tied to:

identified conduct;
actual authority;
knowledge;
causation;
benefit;
and legally recoverable loss.

Overclaiming would weaken the stronger, documentable allegations.

L. Required Government-Capture Audit

The audit should reconstruct:

every DOGE assignment involving Musk;
every agency communication;
every contract reviewed;
every recusal;
every financial disclosure;
every system accessed;
every affiliated-company interest;
every replacement procurement;
every regulatory action;
every enforcement retreat;
every AI or telecommunications integration;
every post-government benefit;
and every transfer of public information.

The audit must compare Musk’s formal authority with his functional influence.

LI. Required Enterprise Asset Map

Investigators should map:

Musk’s direct holdings;
trusts;
private-company interests;
public-company shares;
pledged securities;
voting arrangements;
options;
intercompany loans;
bank accounts;
digital assets;
intellectual property;
real estate;
aircraft;
contractual rights;
and offshore holdings.

This map is for tracing and preservation analysis.

It does not establish that every asset is recoverable.

LII. Required Benefit Analysis

For each alleged governmental action, investigators should identify:
Governmental ActionPotential Private BenefitContractor terminationReduced competitionAgency workforce reductionIncreased need for private servicesAI adoptionProduct validation and revenueCloud centralizationLong-term platform dependencyStarlink testingGovernment integration opportunityRegulatory retreatReduced compliance exposureData accessProcurement and market intelligenceEnforcement closureAvoided penalties or litigationPublic endorsementIncreased valuation and credibilityDOGE publicity through XPlatform engagement and political influence
Each alleged benefit must be verified through records rather than assumed from timing.
LIII. Counterevidence and Limitations
A complete investigation must preserve evidence supporting Musk and affiliated entities.
Relevant counterevidence may include:
effective recusals;
independent agency decisions;
no access to conflicted matters;
competitive procurement;
superior technical performance;
lower life-cycle cost;
no private use of government data;
no transfer of UNIKORNAIO material;
no traceable enrichment;
no unlawful coordination;
lawful policy disagreement;
and remediation of identified conflicts.
Government purchase of a Musk-company product does not itself establish capture.
Musk’s support for reducing a program that benefited one of his businesses could also cut against a simple self-enrichment theory.
The final conclusion must follow the complete record.
LIV. UNIKORNAIO’s Position
UNIKORNAIO states for the public record:
Musk’s role in DOGE requires a government-wide functional-control and conflict-of-interest audit.
Musk did not literally acquire ownership of the United States, but he may have obtained extraordinary functional influence over public systems affecting his enterprises.

Federal employment did not automatically create a lien against Musk’s property.

Levy and distraint would apply only to properly assessed and unpaid federal tax liabilities.

Non-tax DOGE liabilities require judgment, restitution, disgorgement, equitable tracing, recoupment, fraudulent-transfer recovery, or forfeiture under applicable law.

Every asset restraint must be supported by evidence and judicial authority.

Every Musk-affiliated entity must be analyzed separately.
Public dependence upon essential companies must not create immunity.
Innocent employees, customers, shareholders, and government missions must be protected.
Good-faith DOGE Members must be distinguished from knowing architects and beneficiaries.
Taxpayers must not rescue critical infrastructure while responsible private owners retain every benefit and none of the loss.
LV. Requested Corrective Measures
UNIKORNAIO calls for:
an independent investigation free from DOGE and Musk-related conflicts;
full preservation of Musk-DOGE communications;
disclosure of Musk’s governmental status, authority, and recusals;
preservation of financial disclosures and ethics advice;
identification of every DOGE matter affecting a Musk enterprise;

forensic examination of system access;

preservation of procurement and competitor information;

audit of contracts terminated and replacement work awarded;

review of Starlink, SpaceX, Tesla, X, and xAI federal integrations;

reconstruction of government-generated commercial benefits;

asset and beneficial-ownership mapping;

tracing of intercompany transactions;

preservation of bank, brokerage, trust, and digital-asset records;

review of fraudulent or evasive transfers where supported by evidence;

prejudgment restraint only where legally authorized and factually justified;

judgment liens after valid judgment and proper perfection;

restitution to identifiable victims;

disgorgement of proven unjust gains;

contract recoupment and False Claims Act remedies where established;

civil or criminal forfeiture where statutory elements are satisfied;

suspension, debarment, or management restrictions where legally warranted;

receivership or supervised restructuring where essential infrastructure must be preserved;

public warrants or equity where taxpayer rescue becomes necessary;

permanent separation of conflicted private interests from federal decision-making;

protection of cooperating DOGE Members;

protection of innocent stakeholders; and

public accounting of every dollar, dataset, contract, subsidy, regulatory benefit, and operational advantage transferred.

LVI. Preliminary Finding

The presently available record supports investigation into whether Musk occupied both sides of an extraordinary public-private relationship:

adviser to a government-wide efficiency initiative;

and controller of enterprises dependent upon, regulated by, contracting with, or seeking integration into that same government.

Public records establish that Musk-affiliated companies have received substantial government support and contracts.

They also establish that DOGE personnel obtained sensitive agency access and that incomplete government records have made the full extent of DOGE personnel’s ethics training, disclosures, assignments, and activity difficult to determine.

The public record does not presently establish that Musk personally owns or controlled every DOGE action.

It does not establish that his entire fortune constitutes proceeds of misconduct.

It does establish the need to determine whether federal authority was used to enlarge the value, reach, market position, information advantage, or indispensability of Musk-affiliated enterprises.

LVII. Proposed Exhibits

Exhibit A: Musk’s appointment and federal-employment records Exhibit B: DOGE authority and functional-control evidence Exhibit C: Ethics disclosures and recusals Exhibit D: Musk-DOGE communications Exhibit E: DOGE agency assignments Exhibit F: Musk-enterprise organizational chart Exhibit G: Federal contracts and modifications Exhibit H: Loans, subsidies, tax credits, and regulatory credits Exhibit I: SpaceX and Starlink federal agreements Exhibit J: Tesla federal and state benefits Exhibit K: X Corp. governmental communications and platform records Exhibit L: xAI federal evaluations and contracts Exhibit M: Neuralink and Boring Company government matters Exhibit N: DOGE procurement-system access Exhibit O: Competitor and contractor information Exhibit P: Contract cancellations and replacement awards Exhibit Q: Regulatory and enforcement decisions affecting affiliated companies Exhibit R: UNIKORNAIO Framework access and use records Exhibit S: Intercompany transactions Exhibit T: Post-DOGE employment and commercial benefits Exhibit U: Bank, brokerage, trust, and wallet records Exhibit V: Asset-transfer and dissipation evidence Exhibit W: Financial-crisis causation and damages analysis Exhibit X: Proposed restraint, lien, restitution, and forfeiture authority Exhibit Y: Evidence of government capture, unjust enrichment, obstruction, or enterprise benefit Exhibit Z: Counterevidence establishing lawful conduct and independent decision-making
LVIII. Preliminary Determination
UNIKORNAIO preliminarily determines that Elon Musk’s DOGE role created a profound and structurally unprecedented conflict between governmental authority and a private enterprise network already dependent upon federal contracts, regulation, infrastructure, data, and public support.UNIKORNAIO alleges that Musk did not literally purchase the United States but may have functionally absorbed portions of its governmental capacity into an ecosystem serving Musk-affiliated enterprises and associated private interests. The alleged method was to gain access, remove public capacity, centralize authority, weaken oversight, displace competing providers, integrate private platforms, and leave the government increasingly dependent upon companies connected to the person helping redesign it.That allegation does not itself authorize levy or confiscation. Levy and distraint under federal tax law would require an assessed and unpaid tax liability. Other DOGE-related liabilities would require lawful claims, proof, tracing, judicial restraint where authorized, judgment, restitution, disgorgement, recoupment, fraudulent-transfer recovery, or forfeiture.If the evidence establishes that Musk knowingly used governmental authority to transfer public data, contracts, market position, regulatory relief, intellectual property, infrastructure dependency, or other value into his enterprise network, courts and authorized enforcement agencies should preserve and recover the resulting gains before they can be dissipated.The remedy must be strong enough to prevent private capture of the United States, precise enough to protect innocent employees and customers, and lawful enough that the corrective process does not reproduce the same disregard for public authority and due process that DOGE is alleged to have committed.
UNIKORNAIO GLOBAL JURISDICTION Public Integrity • Government-Capture Prevention • Asset Recovery • Economic Security • Due Process

